1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:05,950
Hell Creek  ·  66 million years ago

2
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:12,720
Some nights, one star burns warmer than the rest.

3
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:22,980
The last morning of the Cretaceous began like any other.

4
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:28,250
The lake. The herd. The mist. A world overflowing with life.

5
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,050
Wisp always slept in the curve of Old Horn's frill.

6
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:38,120
She was very old. And she was almost blind.

7
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:45,100
She found her way by his voice. Every chirp was a small light in her dark.

8
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:48,650
Where she drank, he drank.

9
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:54,850
The Burrower: a small furry mammal, and Wisp's oldest rival.

10
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:59,050
It always got away.

11
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:03,720
In the shallows, a white pebble caught the light.

12
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:08,650
He offered it to her. She nudged it back: it is yours.

13
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:12,500
His best treasure went into the old tree.

14
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:16,500
A mother Edmontosaurus with her hatchlings.

15
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:19,600
Wisp had no mother. He had Old Horn.

16
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:24,000
In the daylight sky, a pale new star. No one noticed.

17
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:27,680
The midday heat was hard on old bones.

18
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:35,320
A dragonfly led him deeper into the ferns than he had ever gone.

19
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:40,550
Then the forest went silent.

20
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:45,050
A young Tyrannosaurus. Patient. Hungry.

21
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:50,250
Wisp called for help.

22
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:54,850
Old Horn could not see the hunter. She came anyway.

23
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:01,250
She tracked it by sound alone, turning her broken horn to every step.

24
00:02:03,550 --> 00:02:06,300
The hunter chose easier prey.

25
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:10,300
Her old legs gave way. He did not leave her side.

26
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:14,580
They walked home together, as close as shadows.

27
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:22,250
At dusk, the new star hung over the Saddle, brighter than any planet.

28
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:29,580
Every night, they counted stars. He chirped. She hummed.

29
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:38,680
Then he found the new star, and remembered: she could not see it.

30
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:43,150
So he brought her his best treasure.

31
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:47,950
This time, she kept it.

32
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:51,850
They slept beneath the burning star.

33
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:58,450
Before dawn, the world went silent. Every head turned south.

34
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:04,850
A second sun rose, in the wrong part of the sky.

35
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:09,920
And for the first time in years, Old Horn saw light.

36
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:15,350
Then the ground heaved, and the lake rose.

37
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:23,400
The sky rained glass: rock melted by an asteroid impact

38
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:27,150
three thousand kilometres to the south.

39
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:32,120
Wisp could have hidden. He ran back to her.

40
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:38,580
She sheltered him beneath her great head.

41
00:03:39,350 --> 00:03:42,150
One chirp. One hum.

42
00:03:53,750 --> 00:03:56,680
Afterwards, nothing moved but the ash.

43
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:05,650
Where they lay, the ash lay deepest.

44
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:11,650
But something small had survived.

45
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:18,250
Sixty-six million years of stone buried them.

46
00:04:21,650 --> 00:04:24,450
Montana. Today.

47
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:31,150
They were found together.

48
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:41,950
The stone that ended their world did not choose them.
It did not know their names.

49
00:04:43,050 --> 00:04:50,350
The universe sends no warnings.
Not to stars. Not to species. Not to us.

50
00:04:52,650 --> 00:05:04,350
Nothing is promised beyond this breath.
So stay a little longer by the water.
Hold the ones you love while the light is still soft.

51
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:08,350
SECOND SUN

